# Phase 7: spice-write: ternaries (FINDINGS-spice-read). On a target of
# another family a `?:` in a then-branch is parenthesized when the source
# reads C's grammar (ngspice-45 reads `x>0?y>0?4:8:8` as 4 at x=1, y=-1;
# Xyce, C, gives 8), and on Xyce each ternary `:` has a blank before it:
# Xyce 7.10 reads `p:n` as one hierarchical name ("syntax error in
# expression"), `p :n` as `n`. Run by hand on ngspice-45 and Xyce 7.10.
spice-bench/ngspice/tests/regression/func/func-1.sp xyce moo/moo2 `?P :N` (Xyce refused `?P:N`)
pdk-gf180mcuD/libs.tech/ngspice/sm141064.ngspice xyce `?1e-15 :d_sab` (Xyce refused `1e-15:d_sab`)
pdk-gf180mcuD/libs.tech/ngspice/sm141064.spice xyce `?1e-15 :d_sab` (Xyce refused `1e-15:d_sab`)

# Phase 7: spice-write: zero transient step (FINDINGS-spice-read). Xyce's
# and LTspice's `.tran 0 <stop>` (the simulator chooses the step) is written
# for ngspice and ESPice with the step (stop - start)/50, ngspice's own
# default step cap (manual §11.3.10): ngspice-45 stops on a zero step
# ("TSTEP is invalid, must be greater than zero"). The .tran line alone
# changes; a sample of 25 written decks run on ngspice-45: none stops on TSTEP.
xyce/BUG_1301/rlc_tranline.cir espice .tran 0 T has the step T/50 (ngspice stops on a zero step)
xyce/BUG_1301/rlc_tranline.cir ngspice .tran 0 T has the step T/50 (ngspice stops on a zero step)
